{%- doc -%}
  Every rule for ../design-system/src/react/Button/Button.tsx's classes. Rendered once per page from
  snippets/design-system-bridge.liquid, the same way product-card-bridge is -- so adding it touched
  no native Horizon file, and the CSS is on every page whether or not any particular block put a
  button on it.

  WHY THIS IS NOT A {% raw %}{% stylesheet %}{% endraw %} TAG IN blocks/mionas-button.liquid ANY MORE, which is where it
  lived: a block's stylesheet tag is compiled into compiled_assets/styles.css, and that file is
  served with a `subset` query parameter -- Shopify ships the subset of block CSS the page actually
  needs, judged by which blocks render. blocks/mionas-add-to-cart.liquid does not render
  blocks/mionas-button.liquid (it cannot: that block is an <a> driven by a `link` setting and has no
  way to submit a form). It only wears its classes. So on a collection page, where no Mionas: Button
  block exists, nothing told Shopify these rules were needed and they were dropped -- leaving the
  add-to-cart button with Horizon's own `.button` styling and none of the Mionas modifiers.

  The reported symptom was "Full width does nothing": .mionas-button--full was simply absent, so
  base.css's `.button { width: fit-content }` was the only width rule left and the button hugged its
  label. It still looked like a Mionas button -- Horizon's themed `.button` is also a dark blue pill,
  and the 48px height comes from blocks/mionas-add-to-cart.liquid's own stylesheet, which does ship
  on that page -- which is what made it read as a width bug rather than as missing CSS.

  Do not move individual rules back into a block. The point of the file is that every
  .mionas-button rule sits in ONE sheet: split across two, their relative order would decide every
  tie between them, which is the same class of bug this is fixing.

  Rendered after base.css (theme.liquid links that in the head; the bridge is an inline style block
  further down), so the rules below that tie with base.css on specificity still win on source order
  -- but see the --full comment for why the load-bearing one does not rely on that.
{%- enddoc -%}
{% style %}
  .mionas-button {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: var(--button-base-gap);
    border: 1.5px solid transparent;
    border-radius: var(--button-base-radius);
    font-family: var(--button-base-font-family);
    font-weight: var(--button-base-font-weight);
    cursor: pointer;
    white-space: nowrap;
    text-decoration: none;
    height: var(--button-size-md-height);
    padding: 0 var(--button-size-md-padding);
    font-size: var(--button-size-md-font-size);
  }

  .mionas-button--sm {
    height: var(--button-size-sm-height);
    padding: 0 var(--button-size-sm-padding);
    font-size: var(--button-size-sm-font-size);
  }

  .mionas-button--sm .mionas-button__icon {
    width: var(--button-size-sm-icon-size);
    height: var(--button-size-sm-icon-size);
  }

  .mionas-button--lg {
    height: var(--button-size-lg-height);
    padding: 0 var(--button-size-lg-padding);
    font-size: var(--button-size-lg-font-size);
  }

  .mionas-button--lg .mionas-button__icon {
    width: var(--button-size-lg-icon-size);
    height: var(--button-size-lg-icon-size);
  }

  /* Button.tsx has no fullWidth prop -- the prototype's `full-width` was its own harness prop.
     The Contacto page needs a full-width WhatsApp button, so this is a theme-side addition;
     see the spec's follow-ups about proposing it back to the design system.

     THE SELECTOR IS DOUBLED ON PURPOSE AND MUST NOT BE FLATTENED BACK TO .mionas-button--full.
     base.css:1006-1040 declares `.button { display: grid; width: fit-content }`, and a Mionas button
     can carry that class: snippets/add-to-cart-button.liquid prefixes whatever class attribute it is
     handed with a literal "button", so every product-card add-to-cart button wears both. At one
     class each, `fit-content` and `width: 100%` tie on specificity and only source order separates
     them -- and `fit-content` is precisely the "full width does nothing" symptom. Source order
     favours this file today, but an order-dependent tie is not a win, so the double class settles it
     at (0,2,0). That matters more here than it did in the block: these rules moved EARLIER in the
     cascade when they moved out of compiled_assets/styles.css into this inline block.

     `display` is doubled for the same reason: it ties with .button's `grid`. */
  .mionas-button.mionas-button--full {
    display: flex;
    width: 100%;
  }

  .mionas-button--primary {
    background: var(--button-primary-background);
    color: var(--button-primary-foreground);
  }

  .mionas-button--secondary {
    background: var(--button-secondary-background);
    color: var(--button-secondary-foreground);
  }

  .mionas-button--outline {
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--button-outline-foreground);
    box-shadow: var(--button-outline-shadow);
  }

  .mionas-button--ghost {
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--button-ghost-foreground);
  }

  .mionas-button--danger {
    background: var(--button-danger-background);
    color: var(--button-danger-foreground);
  }

  .mionas-button--inverse-primary {
    background: var(--button-inverse-primary-background);
    color: var(--button-inverse-primary-foreground);
  }

  .mionas-button--inverse-ghost {
    background: transparent;
    color: var(--button-inverse-ghost-foreground);
  }

  .mionas-button[aria-disabled="true"] {
    opacity: var(--button-state-disabled-opacity);
    cursor: default;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  .mionas-button:active {
    transform: scale(var(--button-state-pressed-scale));
    opacity: var(--button-state-pressed-opacity);
  }

  .mionas-button__icon {
    display: inline-flex;
    flex: none;
    width: var(--button-size-md-icon-size);
    height: var(--button-size-md-icon-size);
  }
{% endstyle %}
